<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- DISABLED 2026-11-11 by founder order -- see bin/idp-disk-watch's header for the record.

     This template rendered a KeepAlive resident disk guardian: one bounded reclaim step per
     cycle below its floor, forever. Measured before removal: 120 steps, last_freed_mb=3,
     while the volume fell 1526 MB -> 1012 MB and the machine sat at 26 MB free RAM with 80%
     swap. When the guardian was stopped the volume recovered on its own, 1012 MB -> 2335 MB.

     It also held the executor's 1500 MB disk floor closed against every command, including
     `launchctl bootout` and `rm` on itself: a safeguard that blocks its own off switch.

     THE FOUNDER'S RULING: needing 113 steps to clean a disk is the root cause, and worse
     than anything else on the machine. It does not come back in this shape.

     If a cleanup is ever written again it must not be KeepAlive, must report free MB before
     and after against a target in ONE run, and must never gate its own disable path.
-->
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>ai.estate.disk-watch.retired</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key><array>
    <string>/bin/echo</string>
    <string>ai.estate.disk-watch retired 2026-11-11: no resident disk guardian. See bin/idp-disk-watch.</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>
  <key>Disabled</key><true/>
</dict></plist>
